        VALUES
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'How did Khushwant Singh''s grandmother spend her happiest half-hour of the day in the city house?', '{"A":"Listening to radio","B":"Feeding hundreds of little sparrows in the courtyard with broken bread crumbs","C":"Visiting neighbours","D":"Spinning only"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 1, 'Sparrows perched even on her legs, shoulders, and head without fear.', NULL, NULL, 'Sparrows perched even on her legs, shoulders, and head without fear.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000002', 'What deeply distressed the grandmother about the author''s English medium city school?', '{"A":"Uniform colour","B":"There was no teaching about God and the scriptures, and music was taught","C":"Long bus distance","D":"Hard homework"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 1, 'She associated music with lewd/beggar associations rather than gentlefolk.', NULL, NULL, 'She associated music with lewd/beggar associations rather than gentlefolk.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000003', 'In Shirley Toulson''s "A Photograph", what does the phrase "Both wry / With the laboured ease of loss" convey?', '{"A":"Joyful vacation","B":"Irony that both mother (loss of childhood) and poet (loss of mother''s laughter) bear painful absence with forced calm","C":"Anger","D":"Childhood curiosity"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 1, 'Oxymoron "laboured ease" shows quiet grief.', NULL, NULL, 'Oxymoron "laboured ease" shows quiet grief.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000004', 'Why did thousands of sparrows sit silently scattered around the dead grandmother without chirruping?', '{"A":"They were sick","B":"They sensed her death and mourned silently, ignoring bread crumbs","C":"A cat scared them","D":"It was night"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 1, 'Silent tribute from birds she fed daily.', NULL, NULL, 'Silent tribute from birds she fed daily.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000005', 'In "A Photograph", "Its silence silences" refers to:', '{"A":"Calm beach water","B":"The dead mother''s permanent silence leaves the bereaved poet speechless with grief","C":"Old camera noise","D":"Quiet house"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 1, 'Silence of death hushes all complaint.', NULL, NULL, 'Silence of death hushes all complaint.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000006', 'What was the only day the grandmother skipped her prayers just before passing away?', '{"A":"Her birthday","B":"The evening she collected neighbourhood women to beat an old dilapidated drum celebrating the author''s return home","C":"Diwali night","D":"A rainy morning"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 1, 'She overstrained singing homecoming songs and fell ill with mild fever next morning.', NULL, NULL, 'She overstrained singing homecoming songs and fell ill with mild fever next morning.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000007', 'Contrast the grandmother''s relationship with Khushwant Singh in the village vs the city.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 2, 'In the village she woke him, bathed, walked him to temple school with stale chapattis for village dogs. In the city they shared a room yet drifted apart as he attended motor-bus English school and later got his own university room.', 'Village bond (1M) + City drift (1M)', NULL, 'In the village she woke him, bathed, walked him to temple school with stale chapattis for village dogs. In the city they shared a room yet drifted apart as he attended motor-bus English school and later got his own university room.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000008', 'Why did the poet''s mother laugh looking at the cardboard snapshot taken at age twelve?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 2, 'She amusedly recalled how strangely her parents Betty and Dolly had dressed them up in beach outfits decades earlier — her childhood laughter now exists only as the poet''s memory.', 'Mother''s amusement (1M) + Poet''s poignant memory (1M)', NULL, 'She amusedly recalled how strangely her parents Betty and Dolly had dressed them up in beach outfits decades earlier — her childhood laughter now exists only as the poet''s memory.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000009', 'Explain the poetic contrast between "transient feet" and "terribly transient" in "A Photograph".', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 2, 'The eternal unchanging sea washed human feet that grow old and perish quickly — "transient" highlights mortality against ageless nature.', 'Sea permanence vs human mortality (2M)', NULL, 'The eternal unchanging sea washed human feet that grow old and perish quickly — "transient" highlights mortality against ageless nature.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', '(i) Why did the grandmother take to spinning the wheel quietly after the author got a separate university room? (ii) Describe her appearance ("winter landscape in the mountains"). (iii) How did she pass peacefully? (iv) Give a synonym of "serenity".', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 4, '(i) Accepted her growing seclusion with calm resignation without bitterness. (ii) Silver hair, spotless white clothes, serene peaceful dignity radiating pure calm. (iii) Lying in bed telling beads and praying until rosary slipped silently from lifeless fingers. (iv) Tranquillity / Peacefulness.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Context: "From sunrise to sunset she sat by her wheel spinning and reciting prayers," maintaining dignified calm even as modern life distanced her grandson.', '(i) Accepted her growing seclusion with calm resignation without bitterness. (ii) Silver hair, spotless white clothes, serene peaceful dignity radiating pure calm. (iii) Lying in bed telling beads and praying until rosary slipped silently from lifeless fingers. (iv) Tranquillity / Peacefulness.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b', 'Discuss how "The Portrait of a Lady" celebrates quiet dignity, spiritual devotion, and unspoken inter-generational love.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 4, 'Grandmother never imposes selfish demands; she shifts smoothly from active tutor to silent well-wisher kissing the author''s forehead at departure for abroad, leaving sparrows and prayers as her pure legacy.', 'Dignity (2M) + Inter-generational bond (2M)', NULL, 'Grandmother never imposes selfish demands; she shifts smoothly from active tutor to silent well-wisher kissing the author''s forehead at departure for abroad, leaving sparrows and prayers as her pure legacy.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c', 'Compare the theme of Love, Separation, and Quiet Grief in Khushwant Singh''s prose portrait and Shirley Toulson''s elegiac poem "A Photograph".', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English Core', 'The Portrait of a Lady & A Photograph', 5, 'Both works honour maternal/grandmaternal love etched across time. Khushwant Singh portrays his grandmother''s quiet adaptation to city separation culminating in the unforgettable silent tribute of mourning sparrows. Toulson captures three phases — a girl''s carefree sea holiday, a mother''s nostalgic laughter twenty years later, and finally the poet''s numb silence after her mother''s death.', 'Grandmother comparison (2.5M) + A Photograph phases & synthesis (2.5M)', NULL, 'Both works honour maternal/grandmaternal love etched across time. Khushwant Singh portrays his grandmother''s quiet adaptation to city separation culminating in the unforgettable silent tribute of mourning sparrows. Toulson captures three phases — a girl''s carefree sea holiday, a mother''s nostalgic laughter twenty years later, and finally the poet''s numb silence after her mother''s death.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d', 'What gave the narrator''s family courage inside the battered Wavewalker boat during the Indian Ocean gale?', '{"A":"Extra lifeboats","B":"6-year-old Jonathan saying \"We aren''t afraid of dying if we can all be together\"","C":"A rescue plane overhead","D":"Calm weather forecast"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 1, 'Children''s fearless unity steeled the father''s resolve to fight with everything he had.', NULL, NULL, 'Children''s fearless unity steeled the father''s resolve to fight with everything he had.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', 'How did Sue show extraordinary maturity despite having a swollen head bump and blackened eyes?', '{"A":"She cried loudly","B":"She drew a funny caricature card of Mom and Dad saying \"Here are some funny people\" not to worry them","C":"She hid below deck","D":"She blamed crewmen"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 1, 'Seven-year-old Sue refused to burden her father while he battled leaks.', NULL, NULL, 'Seven-year-old Sue refused to burden her father while he battled leaks.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000f', 'Why did Howard Carter chisel away solidified ritual resins beneath King Tut''s mummy in 1922?', '{"A":"To steal jewels","B":"Resins had hardened like cement gluing the mummy to the solid gold inner coffin","C":"He disliked linen","D":"To wash the tomb"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 1, 'Chiselling damaged bones while freeing the mummy from golden coffin.', NULL, NULL, 'Chiselling damaged bones while freeing the mummy from golden coffin.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', 'What advanced technology did Zahi Hawass use in January 2005 to investigate King Tut''s mystery without harming the mummy?', '{"A":"Microscope slice","B":"Portable Computed Tomography (CT) scanner creating 1,700 digital X-ray cross-sections","C":"Ultrasound only","D":"Carbon fire test"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 1, 'CT scan replaced destructive autopsy to study neck vertebrae, chest, and head.', NULL, NULL, 'CT scan replaced destructive autopsy to study neck vertebrae, chest, and head.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000011', 'Who were Larry Vigil and Herb Seigler aboard Wavewalker?', '{"A":"Journalists","B":"Two experienced crewmen (American and Swiss) hired in Cape Town to tackle the rough Southern Indian Ocean","C":"Coast guard officers","D":"Inspectors"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 1, 'They pumped water furiously like madmen for 36 hours.', NULL, NULL, 'They pumped water furiously like madmen for 36 hours.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000012', 'Who was Amenhotep IV (Akhenaten), Tutankhamun''s controversial predecessor?', '{"A":"A builder of pyramids","B":"Pharaoh who shifted worship to sun disk Aten, smashed Amun images, and moved capital to Akhetaten","C":"A Roman emperor","D":"A Persian king"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 1, 'Tutankhaten later changed his name to Tutankhamun restoring old ways.', NULL, NULL, 'Tutankhaten later changed his name to Tutankhamun restoring old ways.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000013', 'What immediate emergency measures did the narrator take when the gigantic wave smashed Wavewalker''s starboard?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 2, 'He stretched waterproof canvas and secured hatch covers across gaping deck holes, lashed everything down, connected electric backup pumps when hand pumps clogged, and steered toward tiny Île Amsterdam.', 'Canvas repairs + Electric pump & course recalculation (2M)', NULL, 'He stretched waterproof canvas and secured hatch covers across gaping deck holes, lashed everything down, connected electric backup pumps when hand pumps clogged, and steered toward tiny Île Amsterdam.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', 'Why did King Tut''s tomb contain everyday items like board games, bronze razor, linen undergarments, wine, and food?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 2, 'Ancient Egyptians believed royals could carry earthly wealth and daily comforts into the afterlife resurrection journey.', 'Afterlife belief explanation (2M)', NULL, 'Ancient Egyptians believed royals could carry earthly wealth and daily comforts into the afterlife resurrection journey.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'Why was the tiny volcanic island Île Amsterdam described as "the most beautiful island in the world"?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 2, 'Though barely a bleak volcanic rock with little vegetation, it represented miraculous survival and safety for 28 people including crew after terrifying 15-metre waves.', 'Safety & survival relief contrast (2M)', NULL, 'Though barely a bleak volcanic rock with little vegetation, it represented miraculous survival and safety for 28 people including crew after terrifying 15-metre waves.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', '(i) Explain how modern forensic archaeology differs from Howard Carter''s 1922 methods. (ii) Why did a million-dollar CT scanner briefly fail inside the desert trailer? (iii) What constellation stood watch over Tut''s tomb when the mummy returned? (iv) Name two X-ray discoveries made in 1968 and 2005.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 4, '(i) Carter focused on treasures and physically chiselled resins/severed joints; modern archaeology uses non-invasive 3D CT scanning focusing on life & medical death clues. (ii) Sand blew into the cooler fan shutting it down temporarily. (iii) Orion ("soul of Osiris"). (iv) 1968 X-ray found missing breast-bone & front ribs; 2005 CT confirmed head/ribs intact except expected embalming resin.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Context: On 5 January 2005, King Tutankhamun''s 3,300-year-old mummy slid head-first into a high-speed CT scanner while swirling desert winds fanned outside the Valley of the Kings.', '(i) Carter focused on treasures and physically chiselled resins/severed joints; modern archaeology uses non-invasive 3D CT scanning focusing on life & medical death clues. (ii) Sand blew into the cooler fan shutting it down temporarily. (iii) Orion ("soul of Osiris"). (iv) 1968 X-ray found missing breast-bone & front ribs; 2005 CT confirmed head/ribs intact except expected embalming resin.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'Show how teamwork, scientific preparation, and emotional resilience saved everyone aboard Wavewalker.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 4, 'Father''s mathematical dead-reckoning drift calculations, Larry & Herb''s tireless pumping, Mary''s steady wheel-steering during crisis, and Sue & Jon''s calm courage formed an unbreakable team.', 'Adults'' skill (2M) + Children''s morale (2M)', NULL, 'Father''s mathematical dead-reckoning drift calculations, Larry & Herb''s tireless pumping, Mary''s steady wheel-steering during crisis, and Sue & Jon''s calm courage formed an unbreakable team.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', '"Optimism, presence of mind, and scientific ingenuity overcome seemingly impossible crises." Substantiate with detailed incidents from "We''re Not Afraid to Die..." and highlight how scientific tools similarly solved history''s puzzle in "Discovering Tut".', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English Core', 'We''re Not Afraid to Die... & Discovering Tut', 5, 'Trace Wavewalker''s three-year voyage prep, storm tactics (double-lashing, storm jib), emergency hull patchwork, and compass drift correction finding pinprick Île Amsterdam despite broken radio/charts. Link to how Zahi Hawass''s team preserved ancient heritage non-destructively via 0.62-mm CT slices.', 'Wavewalker crisis & science (3M) + Tut forensic advance (2M)', NULL, 'Trace Wavewalker''s three-year voyage prep, storm tactics (double-lashing, storm jib), emergency hull patchwork, and compass drift correction finding pinprick Île Amsterdam despite broken radio/charts. Link to how Zahi Hawass''s team preserved ancient heritage non-destructively via 0.62-mm CT slices.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'In Ted Hughes'' "The Laburnum Top", how does the silent yellow September tree suddenly transform?', '{"A":"Wind blows its leaves","B":"The arrival of a sleek goldfinch awakens the whole tree like a chittering engine of family nestlings","C":"Rain falls on branches","D":"Sun rises"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 1, 'Machine-like tremors of wings and chitterings bring life.', NULL, NULL, 'Machine-like tremors of wings and chitterings bring life.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', 'Why are the Laburnum''s leaves yellowing and its seeds fallen in September?', '{"A":"Disease","B":"Autumn season onset in the afternoon sunlight","C":"Drought","D":"Fire"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 1, 'September afternoon autumn mood contrasts with fiery bird energy.', NULL, NULL, 'September afternoon autumn mood contrasts with fiery bird energy.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'What metaphor describes the goldfinch as she stokes her chicks and darts out to a branch-end?', '{"A":"A candle flame","B":"\"She is the engine of her family\" — then flirts out showing her barred face identity mask","C":"A silver arrow","D":"A falling star"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 1, 'Engine metaphor conveys life-giving energy.', NULL, NULL, 'Engine metaphor conveys life-giving energy.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'In Walt Whitman''s "The Voice of the Rain", what does the rain call itself?', '{"A":"\"I am the tears of sky\"","B":"\"I am the Poem of Earth\"","C":"\"I am the Ocean''s child\"","D":"\"I am winter''s breath\""}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 1, 'Eternal cyclical song returning beauty to its birthplace.', NULL, NULL, 'Eternal cyclical song returning beauty to its birthplace.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'What scientific natural cycle is poetically captured by "Eternal I rise impalpable out of the land and the bottomless sea"?', '{"A":"Nitrogen cycle","B":"Hydrological (Water) cycle: evaporation -> condensation -> rainfall -> rejuvenation","C":"Carbon cycle","D":"Tidal wave"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 1, 'Impalpable (untouchable) water vapour rises and falls as rain.', NULL, NULL, 'Impalpable (untouchable) water vapour rises and falls as rain.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'In the final two lines of "The Voice of the Rain", why does Whitman compare Rain to a Song?', '{"A":"Both make loud noise","B":"Like a song issuing from the poet''s heart, wandering heard or unheard and returning with love, rain nurtures Earth and returns renewed","C":"Both stop quickly","D":"Both are sad"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 1, 'Parallel cycle of artistic creation and nature''s cycle.', NULL, NULL, 'Parallel cycle of artistic creation and nature''s cycle.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'Compare the opening and closing mood of "The Laburnum Top" before and after the goldfinch visits.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 2, 'Opens in dead, silent, still September afternoon ("quite still"); erupts briefly into vibrant chittering life; ends returning to "empty" infinite silence once the bird launches away.', 'Before/During/After contrast (2M)', NULL, 'Opens in dead, silent, still September afternoon ("quite still"); erupts briefly into vibrant chittering life; ends returning to "empty" infinite silence once the bird launches away.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'Why was the poet Walt Whitman amazed when the soft-falling shower replied to his question "Who art thou?"', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 2, 'Nature directly spoke back in personified voice ("strange to tell, gave me an answer"), revealing how Earth''s unseen life cycle depends on eternal rainfall.', 'Personification surprise + Earth dependency (2M)', NULL, 'Nature directly spoke back in personified voice ("strange to tell, gave me an answer"), revealing how Earth''s unseen life cycle depends on eternal rainfall.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', 'Explain the simile "Sleek as a lizard, and alert, and abrupt" in "The Laburnum Top".', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 2, 'Like a quick agile lizard darting into bark crevices, the mother goldfinch slips cautiously into thick branches to guard her chicks from predators.', 'Agility + Caution comparison (2M)', NULL, 'Like a quick agile lizard darting into bark crevices, the mother goldfinch slips cautiously into thick branches to guard her chicks from predators.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', '(i) What does "lave the droughts, atomies, dust-layers of the globe" mean? (ii) What happens to seeds hidden underground without rain? (iii) Explain "reck''d or unreck''d" in the song parallel. (iv) Identify the two poetic devices in "I am the Poem of Earth".', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 4, '(i) Washes and refreshes parched deserts, tiny particles, and dusty surfaces worldwide. (ii) Without rain they remain dormant/unborn latent life. (iii) Whether noticed/appreciated by listeners or ignored, good deeds/art return love to creator. (iv) Personification and Metaphor.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Context: "All that in them without me were seeds only, latent, unborn; And forever, by day and night, I give back life to my own origin, and make pure and beautify it."', '(i) Washes and refreshes parched deserts, tiny particles, and dusty surfaces worldwide. (ii) Without rain they remain dormant/unborn latent life. (iii) Whether noticed/appreciated by listeners or ignored, good deeds/art return love to creator. (iv) Personification and Metaphor.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'Trace the auditory and visual imagery in "The Laburnum Top" ("twitching chirrup", "machine starts up", "tree trembles and thrills").', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 4, 'Initial stillness of yellow autumn light bursts into layered sound (chitterings, trillings) and kinetic vibration (tremor of wings, tree thrilling), creating sensory symphony.', 'Visual (2M) + Auditory & kinetic imagery (2M)', NULL, 'Initial stillness of yellow autumn light bursts into layered sound (chitterings, trillings) and kinetic vibration (tremor of wings, tree thrilling), creating sensory symphony.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', 'Compare how Nature functions as an active rejuvenating force in "The Laburnum Top" (nurturing family energy) and "The Voice of the Rain" (cyclical purification and creative art).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English Core', 'The Laburnum Top & The Voice of the Rain', 5, 'Ted Hughes depicts symbiotic vitality: an ageing silent autumn tree becomes a vibrating shelter ("engine") powered by maternal goldfinch instincts before reverting to stillness. Walt Whitman expands to planetary scale: invisible water vapour rises from land/sea, transforms in clouds, washes dust and awakens dormant seeds, mirroring how pure poetry enriches both world and creator.', 'Laburnum symbiosis (2.5M) + Voice of Rain cycle & art parallel (2.5M)', NULL, 'Ted Hughes depicts symbiotic vitality: an ageing silent autumn tree becomes a vibrating shelter ("engine") powered by maternal goldfinch instincts before reverting to stillness. Walt Whitman expands to planetary scale: invisible water vapour rises from land/sea, transforms in clouds, washes dust and awakens dormant seeds, mirroring how pure poetry enriches both world and creator.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'In Jayant Narlikar''s "The Adventure", how did Prof. Gaitonde explain his transition into an alternate India where Marathas won Panipat?', '{"A":"Magic spell","B":"Catastrophe theory in quantum mechanics / lack of determinism allowing multiple bifurcated histories","C":"Dreaming only","D":"Time machine"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 1, 'Rajendra Deshpande linked Catastrophe theory & quantum non-determinism (many-worlds).', NULL, NULL, 'Rajendra Deshpande linked Catastrophe theory & quantum non-determinism (many-worlds).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'What shocked Prof. Gangadharpant Gaitonde on Hornby Road in alternate Bombay?', '{"A":"Deserted streets","B":"British East India Company HQ, British banks/shops instead of Handloom House in a Bombay where British never ruled India","C":"Flying cars","D":"No trains"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 1, 'India remained independent (Victoria Terminus still stood cleanly via Maratha commercial lease, yet British ruled only Bombay enclave).', NULL, NULL, 'India remained independent (Victoria Terminus still stood cleanly via Maratha commercial lease, yet British ruled only Bombay enclave).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000027', 'What vital page torn from "Bhausahebanchi Bakhar" did Gaitonde produce from his pocket?', '{"A":"A train ticket","B":"The exact account where Vishwasrao survived the bullet graze (\"brushed past his ear\") at Panipat","C":"A bank cheque","D":"A letter from Peshwa"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 1, 'Tiny divergence shifted the 1761 Battle of Panipat outcome.', NULL, NULL, 'Tiny divergence shifted the 1761 Battle of Panipat outcome.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000028', 'Why did Nick Middleton feel sick with blocked nostrils and headaches at Hor beside Lake Manasarovar?', '{"A":"Food poisoning","B":"High-altitude acute hypoxia (~4,760 m) inside dusty cold Darchen/Hor atmosphere","C":"Sunstroke","D":"Car crash"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 1, 'Sleeping in freezing low-oxygen altitude triggered nasal congestion & panic.', NULL, NULL, 'Sleeping in freezing low-oxygen altitude triggered nasal congestion & panic.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000029', 'What parting gift did Lhamo give Nick Middleton at Ravu before his Mount Kailash kora?', '{"A":"A camera","B":"A long-sleeved sheepskin Tibetan coat (drokba coat)","C":"Oxygen tank","D":"A horse"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 1, 'Essential protection against sub-zero plateau winds.', NULL, NULL, 'Essential protection against sub-zero plateau winds.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002a', 'Why did meeting Norbu at Darchen Cafe relieve Nick Middleton deeply?', '{"A":"Norbu gave him medicine","B":"Norbu was also an English-speaking Tibetan academic heading to perform the Kailash kora and shared his lack of yak-riding enthusiasm","C":"Norbu was a doctor","D":"Norbu owned a jeep"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 1, 'Tsetan had left for Lhasa, leaving Nick isolated until Norbu appeared.', NULL, NULL, 'Tsetan had left for Lhasa, leaving Nick isolated until Norbu appeared.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002b', 'Why did Prof. Gaitonde boycott chairing the 1000th Panipat seminar in his real world after returning from Azad Maidan?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 2, 'In the alternate Azad Maidan, audience pelted him with tomatoes/eggs for sitting on the unchaired presidential dais — giving him such shock that he vowed never to preside over public meetings again.', 'Azad Maidan shock (2M)', NULL, 'In the alternate Azad Maidan, audience pelted him with tomatoes/eggs for sitting on the unchaired presidential dais — giving him such shock that he vowed never to preside over public meetings again.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002c', 'Describe how Tibetan mastiffs behaved along the Silk Road camps and why they were prized in imperial China.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 2, 'Massive shaggy black dogs with bright red collars barked furiously and chased Middleton''s jeep fearlessly for 100 m; historically prized in China''s imperial courts as fearless hunting dogs.', 'Appearance/fearlessness (1M) + Historical China role (1M)', NULL, 'Massive shaggy black dogs with bright red collars barked furiously and chased Middleton''s jeep fearlessly for 100 m; historically prized in China''s imperial courts as fearless hunting dogs.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002d', 'Why was the town of Hor described by Middleton as a "grim, miserable place" contrasting with sacred Lake Manasarovar?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 2, 'Though overlooking sacred Manasarovar (famed source of four great Asian rivers), modern Hor had zero vegetation, thick dust, rocks, and scattered garbage — disillusioning earlier poetic expectations.', 'Dirt/dust contrast vs Manasarovar lore (2M)', NULL, 'Though overlooking sacred Manasarovar (famed source of four great Asian rivers), modern Hor had zero vegetation, thick dust, rocks, and scattered garbage — disillusioning earlier poetic expectations.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002e', '(i) What does Catastrophe Theory state as applied by Rajendra Deshpande? (ii) Why didn''t electron energy jumps in quantum physics explain reality deterministically? (iii) How did Marathas run independent India post-1761 in alternate history? (iv) Why was Gaitonde hit by a truck right while pondering Panipat?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 4, '(i) Small sudden shifts in initial conditions (bullet missing by inches) trigger massive bifurcations in system behaviour. (ii) Quantum states lack single deterministic prediction until observed/collapsed. (iii) Modernised industry, science, and reduced East India Company to Bombay commercial outpost before ending rule cleanly by 20th century. (iv) Shock of collision triggered his quantum mind-jump between parallel realities.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Context: Comparing the torn Bakhar page on Rajendra Deshpande''s desk with Prof. Gaitonde''s Azad Maidan trauma sparks a scientific debate blending history and quantum physics.', '(i) Small sudden shifts in initial conditions (bullet missing by inches) trigger massive bifurcations in system behaviour. (ii) Quantum states lack single deterministic prediction until observed/collapsed. (iii) Modernised industry, science, and reduced East India Company to Bombay commercial outpost before ending rule cleanly by 20th century. (iv) Shock of collision triggered his quantum mind-jump between parallel realities.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002f', 'Trace Nick Middleton''s physical & psychological ordeal at Darchen medical clinic and how Tibetan doctor rescued him.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 4, 'Struggling to sleep upright terrified of suffocating from sinus blockage, he visited Darchen Medical College where a Tibetan doctor diagnosed simple cold + altitude and prescribed a five-day brown herbal powder/pellet course yielding instant sound sleep.', 'Symptoms & fear (2M) + Tibetan medicine recovery (2M)', NULL, 'Struggling to sleep upright terrified of suffocating from sinus blockage, he visited Darchen Medical College where a Tibetan doctor diagnosed simple cold + altitude and prescribed a five-day brown herbal powder/pellet course yielding instant sound sleep.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'Contrast the inner intellectual journey of Prof. Gaitonde ("The Adventure") with the physical geographical endurance of Nick Middleton ("Silk Road").', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English Core', 'The Adventure & Silk Road', 5, 'Gaitonde travels mentally through an alternate historical reality — experiencing pride seeing a self-reliant scientific India yet facing comedic humiliation defending presidential chair customs on Azad Maidan. Middleton battles snow-choked passes at 5,515 m, roaring Tibetan mastiffs, dusty Hor, and hypoxic sleeplessness in Darchen until scholarly camaraderie with Norbu restores hope for completing the Kailash kora.', 'Adventure (2.5M) + Silk Road (2.5M)', NULL, 'Gaitonde travels mentally through an alternate historical reality — experiencing pride seeing a self-reliant scientific India yet facing comedic humiliation defending presidential chair customs on Azad Maidan. Middleton battles snow-choked passes at 5,515 m, roaring Tibetan mastiffs, dusty Hor, and hypoxic sleeplessness in Darchen until scholarly camaraderie with Norbu restores hope for completing the Kailash kora.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000031', 'In Markus Natten''s "Childhood", what three turning points mark the speaker losing childhood?', '{"A":"Turning 18","B":"Discovering Hell/Heaven aren''t in Geography, realising adults preach love without practising it, and finding his own independent mind","C":"Leaving home","D":"Graduating"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 1, 'Rationalism, adult hypocrisy, and individuality.', NULL, NULL, 'Rationalism, adult hypocrisy, and individuality.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000032', 'According to the concluding stanza of "Childhood", where did the speaker''s lost childhood go?', '{"A":"To heaven","B":"\"To some forgotten place, that''s hidden in an infant''s face\"","C":"Inside old toys","D":"Into books"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 1, 'Innocence survives only in a baby''s face.', NULL, NULL, 'Innocence survives only in a baby''s face.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000033', 'In Elizabeth Jennings'' "Father to Son", why does the father lament "We speak like strangers"?', '{"A":"They live in different countries","B":"A deep generational communication gap persists even after living together in the same house for years","C":"Son cannot hear","D":"Language barrier"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 1, 'Emotional alienation under one roof.', NULL, NULL, 'Emotional alienation under one roof.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000034', 'What does the father mean by "The seed I spent or sown it where / The land is his and none of mine"?', '{"A":"Farming loss","B":"Though the father raised him, the son now lives in a separate mental/value world the father cannot control or understand","C":"Sold property","D":"Lost savings"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 1, 'Generational worldview divergence.', NULL, NULL, 'Generational worldview divergence.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000035', 'What paradox appears in "Yet have I killed / The seed I spent..." and "Silence surrounds us"?', '{"A":"Physical death","B":"Father wonders if strict/clumsy upbringing killed emotional closeness, leaving awkward domestic silence despite yearning to forgive","C":"Moving cities","D":"Noise at home"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 1, 'Regret mixed with desire for reconciliation.', NULL, NULL, 'Regret mixed with desire for reconciliation.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000036', 'What "prodigal" wish does the father express in "Father to Son"?', '{"A":"To disown the son","B":"He would welcome the son back home like the biblical Prodigal Son rather than see him move away emotionally","C":"To retire abroad","D":"To buy gifts"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 1, 'Unconditional paternal yearning to forgive.', NULL, NULL, 'Unconditional paternal yearning to forgive.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000037', 'Explain what Markus Natten realised about adults when he turned eleven/older ("That they were not all they seemed to be").', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 2, 'He noticed adults constantly talked and preached about love yet rarely acted lovingly in daily life — awakening disillusionment with hypocrisy.', 'Preach vs practice hypocrisy (2M)', NULL, 'He noticed adults constantly talked and preached about love yet rarely acted lovingly in daily life — awakening disillusionment with hypocrisy.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000038', 'Why does the son say "I cannot understand myself, why anger grows from grief" in "Father to Son"?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 2, 'Even the son feels trapped by mutual misunderstanding; deep sorrow over their estrangement erupts involuntarily into defensive anger.', 'Grief turning to anger (2M)', NULL, 'Even the son feels trapped by mutual misunderstanding; deep sorrow over their estrangement erupts involuntarily into defensive anger.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000039', 'Explain "My mind was really mine / To use whichever way I choose" in "Childhood".', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 2, 'Marks intellectual autonomy — forming original thoughts free from echoing others'' opinions, signalling entry into independent adulthood.', 'Individuality & original thought (2M)', NULL, 'Marks intellectual autonomy — forming original thoughts free from echoing others'' opinions, signalling entry into independent adulthood.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003a', '(i) Explain the biblical allusion in "I would have Him prodigal". (ii) Explain "We both must live on the same globe and the same land". (iii) Why do both father and son "put out an empty hand"? (iv) Tone of "Father to Son".', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 4, '(i) Biblical parable of a spendthrift son welcomed joyfully back by his forgiving father. (ii) Recognition that estrangement hurts both when they inhabit the same shared world/family. (iii) Both silently wish to forgive and reconcile yet struggle to bridge pride/distance. (iv) Melancholic, yearning, introspective.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Context: "We both must live on the same globe and the same land, He speaks: I cannot understand myself, why anger grows from grief. We each put out an empty hand, longing for something to forgive."', '(i) Biblical parable of a spendthrift son welcomed joyfully back by his forgiving father. (ii) Recognition that estrangement hurts both when they inhabit the same shared world/family. (iii) Both silently wish to forgive and reconcile yet struggle to bridge pride/distance. (iv) Melancholic, yearning, introspective.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003b', 'Trace how "Childhood" blends Rationalism, Individuality, and Hypocrisy into a universal coming-of-age question.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 4, 'The poet questions WHEN innocence ended (age 11 geography reality test vs witnessing adult insincerity vs independent thinking) before shifting to WHERE it rests — hidden forever inside an infant''s trusting smile.', 'Rationalism/Hypocrisy/Individuality (3M) + Shift to "Where" (1M)', NULL, 'The poet questions WHEN innocence ended (age 11 geography reality test vs witnessing adult insincerity vs independent thinking) before shifting to WHERE it rests — hidden forever inside an infant''s trusting smile.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003c', 'Compare the loss of innocence in "Childhood" with the breakdown and hope of reconciliation in "Father to Son". What message do both poems offer families today?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English Core', 'Childhood & Father to Son', 5, 'Natten captures the inevitable natural transition from unquestioning childhood trust to analytical independence — grieving lost simplicity without blaming parents. Jennings shifts inside the household where an inability to update communication from authority to adult-friendship isolates father and son. Both urge empathy, honest listening, and respecting evolving individuality.', 'Both poems compared (3.5M) + Modern family takeaway (1.5M)', NULL, 'Natten captures the inevitable natural transition from unquestioning childhood trust to analytical independence — grieving lost simplicity without blaming parents. Jennings shifts inside the household where an inability to update communication from authority to adult-friendship isolates father and son. Both urge empathy, honest listening, and respecting evolving individuality.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003d', 'To which Armenian tribe famous for absolute honesty and poverty did Aram and Mourad belong?', '{"A":"Kurdish tribe","B":"Garoghlanian tribe","C":"Cossack tribe","D":"Nomadic tribe"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 1, 'Garoghlanian hallmark: proud, poor, strictly honest.', NULL, NULL, 'Garoghlanian hallmark: proud, poor, strictly honest.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003e', 'Who was the rightful owner of the beautiful white horse riding outside Aram''s window?', '{"A":"Uncle Khosrove","B":"Farmer John Byro","C":"Zorab","D":"Vazire"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 1, 'John Byro, an Assyrian farmer who missed his stolen horse.', NULL, NULL, 'John Byro, an Assyrian farmer who missed his stolen horse.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003f', 'What was Uncle Khosrove''s famous catchphrase dismissing every crisis (even his own house on fire)?', '{"A":"\"Mind your own business\"","B":"\"It is no harm; pay no attention to it\"","C":"\"Everything is lost\"","D":"\"Call the police\""}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 1, 'Roaring dismissal ended every argument.', NULL, NULL, 'Roaring dismissal ended every argument.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000040', 'How did Mourad justify taking John Byro''s white horse without calling it "stealing"?', '{"A":"He paid later","B":"It wouldn''t become stealing unless they offered to sell the horse for money, which they would never do","C":"Byro gifted it","D":"It was stray"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 1, 'Boyish rationalisation preserving family honesty code.', NULL, NULL, 'Boyish rationalisation preserving family honesty code.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000041', 'What convinced John Byro NOT to accuse Aram and Mourad even after inspecting the horse''s teeth tooth-for-tooth?', '{"A":"Horse looked different","B":"The Garoghlanian family''s centuries-old reputation for honesty made him trust his heart over his eyes","C":"Boys ran away","D":"Night was dark"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 1, '"The fame of your family for honesty is well known to me... yet the horse is the twin of my horse."', NULL, NULL, '"The fame of your family for honesty is well known to me... yet the horse is the twin of my horse."', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000042', 'Why did Aram and Mourad secretly return the white horse to John Byro''s vineyard barn?', '{"A":"Police searched town","B":"John Byro''s gentle trust pricked their conscience (plus Aram still couldn''t ride well)","C":"Horse fell sick","D":"Winter started"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 1, 'Moral awakening inspired by Byro''s faith in their family honour.', NULL, NULL, 'Moral awakening inspired by Byro''s faith in their family honour.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000043', 'Why was Aram torn between disbelief and delight when he saw Mourad sitting on a white horse at 4 AM?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 2, 'The Garoghlanians were too poor even to afford food reliably, let alone buy a horse; yet his lifelong longing to ride battled his shock at whether Mourad had stolen it.', 'Poverty vs Riding longing (2M)', NULL, 'The Garoghlanians were too poor even to afford food reliably, let alone buy a horse; yet his lifelong longing to ride battled his shock at whether Mourad had stolen it.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000044', 'What does Mourad mean when he repeatedly claims "I have a way with horses / dogs / farmers"?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 2, 'He possesses natural calm empathy with animals — soothing a wild horse, calming John Byro''s silent dogs, and even healing a young robin''s hurt wing.', 'Gentle animal rapport examples (2M)', NULL, 'He possesses natural calm empathy with animals — soothing a wild horse, calming John Byro''s silent dogs, and even healing a young robin''s hurt wing.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000045', 'Why did Uncle Khosrove shout "It is no harm" when his son Arak ran to the barber shop saying their house was on fire?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 2, 'Khosrove''s enormous, impatient eccentric streak made him dismiss every material loss loudly ("Enough, it is no harm"), mirroring Mourad''s inherited "crazy streak".', 'Eccentric streak explained (2M)', NULL, 'Khosrove''s enormous, impatient eccentric streak made him dismiss every material loss loudly ("Enough, it is no harm"), mirroring Mourad''s inherited "crazy streak".', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000046', '(i) Identify the dramatic tension when Aram and Mourad bump into John Byro while walking the horse. (ii) What name did Mourad call the horse? (iii) How did John Byro inspect the horse? (iv) Why was John Byro overjoyed the next day in his surrey?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 4, '(i) Fear of being exposed as horse thieves right next to Fetvajian''s deserted vineyard. (ii) "My Heart". (iii) Looked into its mouth, counted teeth matching his own missing horse tooth-for-tooth. (iv) His returned horse was stronger and better-tempered than ever before.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Context: Early morning two weeks later, while leading the white horse toward the barn to hide it, Aram and Mourad run straight into farmer John Byro walking back to town.', '(i) Fear of being exposed as horse thieves right next to Fetvajian''s deserted vineyard. (ii) "My Heart". (iii) Looked into its mouth, counted teeth matching his own missing horse tooth-for-tooth. (iv) His returned horse was stronger and better-tempered than ever before.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000047', 'Show how William Saroyan uses gentle humour through Aram''s perspective vs Uncle Khosrove''s loudness.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 4, 'Aram solemnly distinguishes riding-joy from "stealing for money" while falling clumsily into irrigation ditches; Khosrove silences weeping John Byro ("You have legs!") simply because he misses his homeland.', 'Aram innocence (2M) + Khosrove humour (2M)', NULL, 'Aram solemnly distinguishes riding-joy from "stealing for money" while falling clumsily into irrigation ditches; Khosrove silences weeping John Byro ("You have legs!") simply because he misses his homeland.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000048', '"Trust is far more powerful than accusation in reform." Justify this statement with close reference to John Byro''s confrontation with Aram and Mourad and the Garoghlanian honesty code.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English Core', 'The Summer of the Beautiful White Horse', 5, 'When John Byro recognises his stolen horse tooth-for-tooth, a harsh accusation would have shamed the boys or hardened them into denial. Instead, choosing to honour their Garoghlanian integrity ("A suspicious man would believe his eyes instead of his heart") instantly touches the boys'' conscience, prompting them to return the horse stronger and better-tempered next dawn.', 'Confrontation & Byro''s dignity (3M) + Conscience impact & return (2M)', NULL, 'When John Byro recognises his stolen horse tooth-for-tooth, a harsh accusation would have shamed the boys or hardened them into denial. Instead, choosing to honour their Garoghlanian integrity ("A suspicious man would believe his eyes instead of his heart") instantly touches the boys'' conscience, prompting them to return the horse stronger and better-tempered next dawn.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000049', 'In Marga Minco''s "The Address", why did the narrator visit Number 46, Marconi Street after the war?', '{"A":"To collect rent","B":"To see and retrieve her mother Mrs. S''s cherished silver, antiques, and tablecloths entrusted during the war","C":"To meet an old classmate","D":"To sell a house"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 1, 'Mrs. Dorling had carried away Mrs. S''s belongings "for safekeeping".', NULL, NULL, 'Mrs. Dorling had carried away Mrs. S''s belongings "for safekeeping".', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004a', 'How did Mrs. Dorling treat the narrator on her first visit after the war?', '{"A":"Welcomed her warmly","B":"Refused to recognise her, kept the door half-shut, and wore Mrs. S''s green knitted cardigan","C":"Returned all silver","D":"Invited her to tea"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 1, 'Cold denial mixed with guilt while wearing the mother''s cardigan.', NULL, NULL, 'Cold denial mixed with guilt while wearing the mother''s cardigan.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004b', 'Why did the narrator ultimately resolve to FORGET the address 46, Marconi Street?', '{"A":"Train was cancelled","B":"Separated from her late mother and original surroundings, the familiar silver/pewter seemed strange, oppressive, and meaningless in Dorling''s tasteless room","C":"Police barred entry","D":"Items were broken"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 1, 'Moving forward healed war trauma without clinging to material relics.', NULL, NULL, 'Moving forward healed war trauma without clinging to material relics.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004c', 'In J.B. Priestley''s "Mother''s Day", how does Mrs. Fitzgerald help timid Mrs. Annie Pearson?', '{"A":"By calling police","B":"By swapping personalities temporarily via Eastern magic to firmly confront George, Doris, and Cyril","C":"By writing a letter","D":"By cooking dinner"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 1, 'Bold Mrs. Fitzgerald sets clear household boundaries.', NULL, NULL, 'Bold Mrs. Fitzgerald sets clear household boundaries.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004d', 'What shocked Doris Pearson most when she returned home from work?', '{"A":"Broken windows","B":"Her gentle mother calmly smoking, playing patience cards, and refusing to iron her yellow silk dress","C":"Empty fridge","D":"Visitors sleeping"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 1, 'Role-reversal comedy exposes how family exploited Annie''s kindness.', NULL, NULL, 'Role-reversal comedy exposes how family exploited Annie''s kindness.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004e', 'What nickname at the club humiliated George Pearson when bold "Mrs. Pearson" revealed it?', '{"A":"Lazy George","B":"\"Pompy-ompy Pearson\" because he was slow and pompous","C":"Quiet George","D":"Captain"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 1, 'Forced George to see he commanded zero respect outside while neglecting Annie.', NULL, NULL, 'Forced George to see he commanded zero respect outside while neglecting Annie.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004f', 'Why did Mrs. S feel grateful rather than suspicious when Mrs. Dorling kept carrying suitcases of silver and crockery away?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 2, 'Fearing Nazi deportation and losing everything, trusting Mrs. S believed Mrs. Dorling was risking herself kindly to save their heirloom belongings for after the war.', 'War fear & misplaced gratitude (2M)', NULL, 'Fearing Nazi deportation and losing everything, trusting Mrs. S believed Mrs. Dorling was risking herself kindly to save their heirloom belongings for after the war.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000050', 'What burn mark on a woollen tablecloth helped prove to the narrator on her second visit that she was sitting amid her own family''s things?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 2, 'While Mrs. Dorling''s teenage daughter poured tea from a white pot with gold border, the narrator followed the burn mark that had never been repaired on their woollen tablecloth.', 'Burn mark identification (2M)', NULL, 'While Mrs. Dorling''s teenage daughter poured tea from a white pot with gold border, the narrator followed the burn mark that had never been repaired on their woollen tablecloth.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000051', 'State two ways Mrs. Pearson''s family members (Doris, Cyril, George) took her labour for granted before the personality swap.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 2, 'Expected unpaid 24/7 cooking, ironing, tea-service, and errands without "thank you", while ignoring her weekends and mocking her staying home.', '2 clear examples × 1M', NULL, 'Expected unpaid 24/7 cooking, ironing, tea-service, and errands without "thank you", while ignoring her weekends and mocking her staying home.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000052', '(i) Compare Mrs. Pearson''s behaviour before vs after the personality swap. (ii) Why does Mrs. Fitzgerald warn Annie "Don''t go soft again" after switching back? (iii) What new family routine does Annie propose at the end? (iv) How does George react humbly at the play''s close?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 4, '(i) Timid apologetic drudge -> calm assertive confident boundary-setter. (ii) Habitual submissiveness could undo the shock therapy unless Annie maintains firmness. (iii) Family stays home playing Rummy together while kids help cook supper. (iv) Speaks quietly and accepts staying in warmly.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Context: After watching Mrs. Fitzgerald in Annie''s body tell off Cyril, Doris, and George sharply, Annie begs to switch bodies back quickly before their feelings break completely.', '(i) Timid apologetic drudge -> calm assertive confident boundary-setter. (ii) Habitual submissiveness could undo the shock therapy unless Annie maintains firmness. (iii) Family stays home playing Rummy together while kids help cook supper. (iv) Speaks quietly and accepts staying in warmly.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000053', 'Explain why war survivors in "The Address" found familiar belongings strangely alien after losing loved ones.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 4, 'Objects gain emotional warmth from the home and people who arranged them; seeing family Hanukkah candleholders and cutlery dumped haphazardly inside a musty room amid ungrateful strangers turns nostalgia sour — freeing the narrator to leave both objects and address behind.', 'Emotional context of objects (2M) + Liberation decision (2M)', NULL, 'Objects gain emotional warmth from the home and people who arranged them; seeing family Hanukkah candleholders and cutlery dumped haphazardly inside a musty room amid ungrateful strangers turns nostalgia sour — freeing the narrator to leave both objects and address behind.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000054', 'Contrast how dignity and self-respect are reclaimed differently in "The Address" (quiet inner detachment from war loss) and "Mother''s Day" (humorous assertive confrontation at home).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English Core', 'The Address & Mother''s Day', 5, 'Marga Minco''s narrator refuses to beg or fight greedy Mrs. Dorling once she realises memories of her mother transcend silver cutlery, reclaiming peace by letting go of 46 Marconi Street. J.B. Priestley uses witty domestic farce so overworked Annie Pearson, guided by Mrs. Fitzgerald, insists her husband and adult children share chores and treat her as an equal human being.', 'The Address detachment (2.5M) + Mother''s Day assertion (2.5M)', NULL, 'Marga Minco''s narrator refuses to beg or fight greedy Mrs. Dorling once she realises memories of her mother transcend silver cutlery, reclaiming peace by letting go of 46 Marconi Street. J.B. Priestley uses witty domestic farce so overworked Annie Pearson, guided by Mrs. Fitzgerald, insists her husband and adult children share chores and treat her as an equal human being.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000055', 'In A.J. Cronin''s "Birth", who were waiting anxiously for Dr. Andrew Manson at Blaenelly?', '{"A":"His fiancee Christine","B":"Joe Morgan and his elderly mother (after 20 years of marriage awaiting their first baby)","C":"Hospital matron","D":"Police officer"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 1, 'First child after nearly 20 years of marriage.', NULL, NULL, 'First child after nearly 20 years of marriage.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000056', 'When the newborn baby appeared pale, limp, and lifeless from asphyxia pallida, how did Dr. Andrew revive it?', '{"A":"Injections only","B":"Alternated hot and cold water plunges + gentle rhythmic chest compression (taught from a medical journal/old practice)","C":"Surgery","D":"Left it aside"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 1, 'Special method of alternating hot/cold basins + rubbing with rough towel brought the miracle cry.', NULL, NULL, 'Special method of alternating hot/cold basins + rubbing with rough towel brought the miracle cry.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000057', 'What conflict weighted Andrew Manson''s mind before entering Joe Morgan''s house?', '{"A":"Lost money","B":"Disappointment over a bitter evening quarrel with Christine in Cardiff, contrasting ideal marriage vs failed marriages around him","C":"Broken bike","D":"Fever"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 1, 'Personal gloom faded as medical duty took over.', NULL, NULL, 'Personal gloom faded as medical duty took over.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000058', 'In Vikram Seth''s "The Tale of Melon City", why did the "just and placid" King order an arch built across the thoroughfare?', '{"A":"Military parade","B":"To edify spectators spiritually — though it accidentally knocked his crown off because it was built too low","C":"Tax collection","D":"Flood barrier"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 1, 'Farcical chain of blame followed.', NULL, NULL, 'Farcical chain of blame followed.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000059', 'Who ended up getting hanged at the royal arch in "The Tale of Melon City"?', '{"A":"The chief builder","B":"The King himself (because only he was tall enough to fit the noose height set earlier!)","C":"The wise man","D":"The architect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 1, 'Satisfied the crowd''s demand for "someone hanged" and King''s literal justice.', NULL, NULL, 'Satisfied the crowd''s demand for "someone hanged" and King''s literal justice.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005a', 'Why did the citizens of Melon City calmly accept a Melon as their new King?', '{"A":"Melons were rare","B":"As long as the King left them in peace and liberty (\"laissez faire\"), they didn''t care who wore the crown","C":"It was a festival","D":"The melon spoke"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 1, 'Satire on blind literalism and public apathy toward governance.', NULL, NULL, 'Satire on blind literalism and public apathy toward governance.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005b', 'Describe the critical dilemma Dr. Andrew Manson faced moments after delivering Joe Morgan''s baby.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 2, 'Susan Morgan lay collapsing with feeble pulse needing immediate maternal stimulant injection, while the newborn lay motionless and white with asphyxia pallida — forcing instant triage saving mother first, then fighting desperately for the child.', 'Mother crisis (1M) + Baby resuscitation triage (1M)', NULL, 'Susan Morgan lay collapsing with feeble pulse needing immediate maternal stimulant injection, while the newborn lay motionless and white with asphyxia pallida — forcing instant triage saving mother first, then fighting desperately for the child.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005c', 'Why did Dr. Andrew say "Oh, God, I''ve done something real at last" walking home at 5 AM?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 2, 'Despite personal heartache and exhaustion, saving both Susan and her long-awaited baby gave him profound medical fulfilment beyond textbooks.', 'Emotional & professional triumph (2M)', NULL, 'Despite personal heartache and exhaustion, saving both Susan and her long-awaited baby gave him profound medical fulfilment beyond textbooks.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005d', 'Trace the comical chain of blame in "The Tale of Melon City" from Chief Builder to the Wisest Man.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 2, 'King blamed Chief Builder -> blamed Workmen -> blamed wrong-sized Bricks -> blamed Mason -> blamed Architect -> Architect reminded King HE made the amendments -> panicked King summoned blind, deaf "wisest man" who declared the Arch culprit!', 'Sequential farce chain (2M)', NULL, 'King blamed Chief Builder -> blamed Workmen -> blamed wrong-sized Bricks -> blamed Mason -> blamed Architect -> Architect reminded King HE made the amendments -> panicked King summoned blind, deaf "wisest man" who declared the Arch culprit!', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005e', '(i) What condition was the newborn suffering from ("white, soft as tallow, cord still white")? (ii) Why did the midwife place the baby beneath the bed on sodden newspaper? (iii) How long did Andrew work frantically until signs of hopelessness set in? (iv) Describe the exact miracle moment.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 4, '(i) Asphyxia pallida (oxygen deprivation). (ii) She believed the child was stillborn. (iii) Nearly half an hour of ceaseless hot-cold dipping. (iv) Tiny chest gave a short convulsive heave, skin turned pink, and a sudden cry burst out.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Context: Torn between despair and sheer stubbornness, Dr. Andrew grabbed a basin of icy cold water and one of steaming hot water, plunging the limp child back and forth while rubbing its chest.', '(i) Asphyxia pallida (oxygen deprivation). (ii) She believed the child was stillborn. (iii) Nearly half an hour of ceaseless hot-cold dipping. (iv) Tiny chest gave a short convulsive heave, skin turned pink, and a sudden cry burst out.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005f', 'What political traits of autocracy, bureaucracy, and passive citizenship does Vikram Seth satirise in "The Tale of Melon City"?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 4, 'Satirises rulers confusing blind procedure with justice ("Crown must be avenged"), spineless sycophantic ministers, and a fickle mob placated by any execution and happy to worship a melon if left undisturbed.', 'King/Ministers satire (2M) + Mob & Laissez-faire (2M)', NULL, 'Satirises rulers confusing blind procedure with justice ("Crown must be avenged"), spineless sycophantic ministers, and a fickle mob placated by any execution and happy to worship a melon if left undisturbed.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000060', 'Contrast dedicated human responsibility in A.J. Cronin''s "Birth" with absurd shirking of responsibility in Vikram Seth''s "The Tale of Melon City".', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English Core', 'Birth & The Tale of Melon City', 5, 'Dr. Andrew Manson refuses to walk away even when experienced midwives pronounce the newborn dead; he shoulders full medical duty under agonising exhaustion, proving skill plus perseverance saves lives. Conversely, in Melon City every official — king, architect, builder, mason — dodges responsibility until absurd logic hangs the monarch and crowns a melon.', 'Andrew''s grit (2.5M) + Melon City satire contrast (2.5M)', NULL, 'Dr. Andrew Manson refuses to walk away even when experienced midwives pronounce the newborn dead; he shoulders full medical duty under agonising exhaustion, proving skill plus perseverance saves lives. Conversely, in Melon City every official — king, architect, builder, mason — dodges responsibility until absurd logic hangs the monarch and crowns a melon.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000061', 'Identify the clause type: "The book **that you lent me yesterday** is fascinating."', '{"A":"Noun clause","B":"Adjective (Relative) clause modifying \"The book\"","C":"Adverb clause of time","D":"Principal clause"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 1, 'Describes the noun "book" using relative pronoun "that".', NULL, NULL, 'Describes the noun "book" using relative pronoun "that".', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000062', 'Fill in the blank: If she _____ earlier, she would not have missed the morning flight.', '{"A":"arrives","B":"had arrived","C":"arrived","D":"will arrive"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 1, 'Type-3 Conditional: If + Past Perfect, would have + V3.', NULL, NULL, 'Type-3 Conditional: If + Past Perfect, would have + V3.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000063', 'Rearrange into meaningful sentence: (A) a balanced diet / (B) essential / (C) is / (D) for good health / (E) and regular exercise', '{"A":"A E C B D","B":"D A C B E","C":"B C A E D","D":"A C B E D"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 1, 'A balanced diet and regular exercise is essential for good health.', NULL, NULL, 'A balanced diet and regular exercise is essential for good health.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000064', 'Choose the correct verb form: Hardly _____ the station when the train steamed in.', '{"A":"we had reached","B":"had we reached","C":"did we reached","D":"we reached"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 1, 'Negative adverb "Hardly" at start triggers subject-verb inversion ("had we reached").', NULL, NULL, 'Negative adverb "Hardly" at start triggers subject-verb inversion ("had we reached").', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000065', 'Identify the Noun Clause in: "What he proposed at the board meeting surprised everyone."', '{"A":"surprised everyone","B":"What he proposed at the board meeting (subject of verb \"surprised\")","C":"at the board meeting","D":"None"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 1, 'Acts as the subject noun of "surprised".', NULL, NULL, 'Acts as the subject noun of "surprised".', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000066', 'Choose the right tense: Look at those dark clouds! It _____ soon.', '{"A":"is going to rain","B":"rained","C":"has rained","D":"was raining"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 1, 'Immediate visible evidence uses "is going to".', NULL, NULL, 'Immediate visible evidence uses "is going to".', 'EASY', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000067', 'Rearrange the following two jumbled word sets into coherent English sentences:
(a) climate / urgent / action / demands / global / change
(b) library / silence / strict / maintained / be / must / inside / the', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 2, '(a) Global climate change demands urgent action.
(b) Strict silence must be maintained inside the library.', '1M each', NULL, '(a) Global climate change demands urgent action.
(b) Strict silence must be maintained inside the library.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000068', 'Transform using a Relative (Adjective) Clause: "I met an old scientist. His invention saved thousands of rural patients."', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 2, 'I met an old scientist whose invention saved thousands of rural patients.', 'Correct "whose" link (2M)', NULL, 'I met an old scientist whose invention saved thousands of rural patients.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11051111-0000-4000-8000-000000000069', 'Fill correct tenses:
(a) Water _____ (boil) at 100°C at sea level.
(b) While I _____ (walk) along the beach, I found a rare conch shell.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'English Core', 'Integrated Grammar (Tenses, Clauses & Reordering)', 2, '(a) boils (universal scientific truth); (b) was walking (interrupted past continuous action).', '1M each', NULL, '(a) boils (universal scientific truth); (b) was walking (interrupted past continuous action).', 'MEDIUM', true),
